WEBVTT

00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:04.540
Public repositories can provide legitimate technical context through source code,

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:07.990
commit history, issue discussions, documentation, configuration examples,

00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:09.385
and release records.

00:00:09.385 --> 00:00:13.395
Package registries and container references can reveal public dependencies, versions,

00:00:13.395 --> 00:00:15.770
publishers, and distribution relationships.

00:00:15.770 --> 00:00:19.790
Documentation may identify domains, endpoints, product names, deployment patterns,

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:21.385
or third-party services.

00:00:21.385 --> 00:00:25.470
Example configurations can clarify architecture, but they may be outdated, generic,

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:27.140
or unrelated to a live environment.

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:29.257
Preserve provenance and publication dates.

00:00:29.257 --> 00:00:33.425
Dependency clues help defenders understand software supply-chain exposure and identify

00:00:33.425 --> 00:00:35.300
components that need internal verification.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:37.760
A package name alone does not prove deployment.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:39.600
Forks, mirrors, abandoned projects,

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.600
and similarly named packages require careful identity checks.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:47.300
Accidental public exposure of a secret, token, or credential must be handled responsibly.

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:48.555
Do not test or use it.

00:00:48.555 --> 00:00:52.240
Preserve only the minimum evidence needed under policy, restrict access,

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:55.115
notify the authorized owner or approved reporting channel,

00:00:55.115 --> 00:00:56.972
and support revocation and cleanup.

00:00:56.972 --> 00:00:59.560
Repositories and registries have access rules.

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:03.380
Public technical analysis does not authorize entry into private projects,

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:06.480
use of leaked credentials, or retrieval of restricted artifacts.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:10.395
Stop at access boundaries and seek guidance when ownership or reporting authority is

00:01:10.395 --> 00:01:10.975
unclear.

00:01:10.975 --> 00:01:14.660
Report code and package findings as evidence with confidence and limitations.

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:18.780
Connect them to internal inventories through authorized teams before asserting operational

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:19.060
use,

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:20.760
ownership, or vulnerability.
